{"kind":"artifact","text":"The citation guard at its real boundary, internal/app on exact head 9cd8e8f0aa4f877b8213283d1f35cc687e500a77. BuildActRequest refuses a supersession whose target the documentation still cites, so every surface that reaches the log passes the check. It previously lived only in cmd/gs, and the MCP surface submits a supersession directly, so a retirement filed there skipped it — which is how main went red today.\n\nAct.CitedOK carries the migration escape, set only by surfaces that can put the choice to a person. gs batch keeps its whole-file pre-flight, calling the same exported guard, so a batch that cannot land cleanly still lands nothing; dollar-label batch targets, which the pre-flight cannot resolve, reach the boundary check for the first time.\n\nVerified by mutation: removing the boundary check fails the new internal/app test and both cmd/gs tests, because there is one implementation where there were three.\n\nIt rests on 24d800c0 at cmd/gs, which carries the guard this change relocates, and which is live and not stale. This replaces b61b3b0c, which rested on the assert 7f4416ae and was stale from birth: that assert rests on the red-main repair artifact, which rests on 8aa25919, which rests on the retired 66b6cb0b. Resting a candidate on the record that reports a problem drags in everything that problem touched — the fourth time today that a prescribed-looking basis made a head unmergeable.","body":{"commit":"9cd8e8f0aa4f877b8213283d1f35cc687e500a77","path":"internal/app"}}